      "efsm.compare.note": "Guards prune {pruned} of {total} graph paths as infeasible \u2014 those tests would waste effort chasing inputs that cannot exist.",
      "efsm.bridge.symbex.title": "Open the Symbolic Execution Explorer \u2014 path feasibility is exactly a path-condition satisfiability check.",
      "efsm.bridge.logic.title": "Open the Logic Coverage Explorer \u2014 a guard is a predicate, and clause binding solves it the same way.",
      "efsm.quiz.prompt": "An abstract path collects the guards amount \u2265 1, amount \u2264 100, and amount \u2265 200. Why is it infeasible?",
      "efsm.quiz.a": "Because it visits the Denied state, which has no outgoing transition.",
      "efsm.quiz.b": "Because the path is longer than the number of states in the model.",
      "efsm.quiz.c": "Because no single amount can satisfy amount \u2264 100 and amount \u2265 200 at once \u2014 the guard conjunction is unsatisfiable.",
      "efsm.quiz.d": "Because any EFSM with a data variable always has infeasible paths.",
      "efsm.quiz.correct": "Correct. Feasibility is satisfiability of the guard conjunction; amount \u2264 100 \u2227 amount \u2265 200 has no solution, so no test can exercise the path.",
      "efsm.quiz.wrong": "Not quite. The path is infeasible because its guard conjunction is unsatisfiable \u2014 amount cannot be both \u2264 100 and \u2265 200 \u2014 not because of its length or which states it visits.",
      "efsm.lab.prompt": "An EFSM costs more to build and analyze than a plain FSM \u2014 guards, data variables, a constraint solver. What do you get back for that extra cost? When is a plain FSM the better modeling choice?",
      // Usage-Model Statistical Testing Explorer (L5)
      "mbtTab.usage": "Usage-Model Testing",
      "usage.title": "Usage-Model Statistical Testing Explorer",
      "usage.desc": "A usage model is a Markov chain: each transition carries an operational probability. Tests are random walks weighted by that profile, so effort tracks real usage \u2014 and the chain yields the expected test length and a usage-weighted reliability estimate.",
      "usage.model.label": "Usage model (operational profile)",
      "usage.col.from": "From",
      "usage.col.event": "Event",
      "usage.col.prob": "Prob.",
      "usage.col.to": "To",
      "usage.sum.note": "Each state's outgoing probabilities sum to 1. Rows shaded yellow are rare transitions (p < 0.15).",
      "usage.explen": "Expected test-case length: {n} transitions (absorbing Markov chain).",
      "usage.run.label": "Generate & evaluate tests",
      "usage.n.label": "Number of tests:",
      "usage.run": "Run tests \u25B6",
      "usage.hint": "Pick a test count and run \u2014 each test is a probability-weighted random walk from Home to Exit.",
      "usage.reliability.title": "Usage-weighted reliability estimate",
      "usage.reliability.detail": "{pass} passed \xB7 {fail} failed (hit the buggy transition) of {n} tests.",
      "usage.coverage.title": "Transition coverage tally",
      "usage.samples.title": "Sample generated tests",
      "usage.rare.title": "\u26A0 Rare-path warning",
      "usage.rare.desc": "These transitions have low operational probability, so statistical testing under-samples them. If a rare path is also high-risk, supplement the profile with risk-based testing.",
      "usage.rare.item": "rarely walked from {from} to {to} \u2014 few generated tests will ever exercise it.",
      "usage.bridge.rbt.title": "Open the Risk-Based Testing Explorer \u2014 rare-but-risky paths need risk weighting, not just usage weighting.",
      "usage.bridge.pbt.title": "Open the Property-Based Testing Explorer \u2014 both sample inputs from a distribution rather than enumerating them.",
      "usage.quiz.prompt": "A usage model is a Markov chain. How do you compute the expected length of a test case it generates?",
      "usage.quiz.a": "Count the transitions in the model \u2014 that is the expected length.",
      "usage.quiz.b": "Multiply the number of states by the average transition probability.",
      "usage.quiz.c": "Solve E[s] = 1 + \u03A3 p(s\u2192s\u2032)\xB7E[s\u2032] for every state \u2014 the absorbing-chain expected number of steps from the start state.",
      "usage.quiz.d": "It cannot be computed analytically; you must measure it from generated tests.",
      "usage.quiz.correct": "Correct. The expected length is the absorbing Markov chain's expected steps: solve the linear recurrence E[s] = 1 + \u03A3 p(s\u2192s\u2032)\xB7E[s\u2032].",
      "usage.quiz.wrong": "Not quite. The expected length comes from the absorbing-chain recurrence E[s] = 1 + \u03A3 p(s\u2192s\u2032)\xB7E[s\u2032] \u2014 loops and probabilities make it more than a plain transition count.",
      "usage.lab.prompt": "Statistical testing weights tests by the operational profile. If the profile is estimated wrong \u2014 a rare path is actually common, or vice versa \u2014 how does the reliability estimate skew, and which bugs end up under-tested?",
