      "modelmut.contrast": "Model-level mutation works at a higher abstraction than I-series code-level mutation: mutants are FSM edits, not source edits, and a killed mutant proves the suite distinguishes model behaviours \u2014 not that it covers code branches.",
      "modelmut.bridge.specmut.title": "Open the Spec Mutation Explorer \u2014 mutation applied to a specification rather than to a state model.",
      "modelmut.bridge.equivmutant.title": "Open the Equivalent Mutant Explorer (I1) \u2014 the equivalent-mutant problem at the code level.",
      "modelmut.quiz.prompt": "A model-mutant survived the whole test suite. What are the two possible explanations?",
      "modelmut.quiz.a": "The mutant must be equivalent \u2014 a survivor is always an equivalent mutant.",
      "modelmut.quiz.b": "The suite must be too weak \u2014 a survivor always means a missing test.",
      "modelmut.quiz.c": "Either the suite is too weak to reach or distinguish the mutation, or the mutant is equivalent and no test can ever kill it.",
      "modelmut.quiz.d": "The base model is wrong; survivors mean the original FSM has a defect.",
      "modelmut.quiz.correct": "Correct. A survivor is either a suite gap (fixable by adding tests) or an equivalent mutant (unkillable by definition) \u2014 and telling the two apart needs human analysis.",
      "modelmut.quiz.wrong": "Not quite. A survivor has two causes: a suite too weak to expose the mutation, or an equivalent mutant whose behaviour is identical to the base \u2014 so no test can ever kill it.",
      "modelmut.lab.prompt": "A model-mutation score of 100% means the suite distinguishes every model-mutant. Does that guarantee a high code-level mutation score? Why or why not \u2014 what does the model abstraction leave out?",
